Ladybird sizes an absolutely positioned table from its rows and its
specified height alone, so a containing block shorter than the table
never clamps it, and insets on both sides don’t stretch it. Blink,
WebKit and Gecko all do the same. In Blink, OutOfFlowLayoutPart::
TryCalculateOffset() fits a table to its content even with both insets
set, and ComputeOofBlockDimensions() takes the block size of the laid-
out table fragment, which TableLayoutAlgorithm::ComputeRows() grows
past a smaller specified height. WebKit, for its part, uses RenderBox::
computeOutOfFlowPositionedLogicalHeight() to floor the positioned
height at the table’s own content extent. And in Gecko, nsIFrame::
ComputeAbsolutePosAutoSize() keeps table wrappers from stretching, and
nsTableFrame::CalcDesiredBSize() treats any specified height as just a
minimum height for the table.
